### What is `Stream.reduce` with a single binary operator?

`Stream.reduce(BinaryOperator<T> op)` with no identity folds the stream: it applies the operator to the running result and each next element, and returns an `Optional<T>`. The result is empty when the stream has no elements, because there is nothing to fold.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class ReduceBasics {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Optional<Integer> sum = List.of(10, 20, 30).stream()
                .reduce((a, b) -> a + b);
        System.out.println(sum); // Optional[60]

        Optional<Integer> none = List.<Integer>of().stream()
                .reduce((a, b) -> a + b);
        System.out.println(none); // Optional.empty
    }
}
```

```text
Optional[60]
Optional.empty
```

### What is `BinaryOperator.maxBy`?

`BinaryOperator.maxBy(comparator)` returns an operator that, given two elements, returns the greater according to the comparator. Using it as the reduce operator makes each fold step keep the larger element, so the final result is the maximum of the whole stream.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import com.bank.model.Employee;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.function.BinaryOperator;

public class HighestPaidReduce {
    public Optional<Employee> highestPaid(List<Employee> employees) {
        Comparator<Employee> bySalary = Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary);
        return employees.stream().reduce(BinaryOperator.maxBy(bySalary));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = List.of(
                new Employee(1, "Rajesh", "Trading", new BigDecimal("1200000")),
                new Employee(2, "Priya", "Risk", new BigDecimal("850000")),
                new Employee(3, "Pankaj", "Management", new BigDecimal("2000000"))
        );
        new HighestPaidReduce().highestPaid(employees)
                .ifPresent(e -> System.out.println(e.name() + " " + e.salary()));
    }
}
```

```text
Pankaj 2000000
```

The `Employee` record:

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record Employee(long id, String name, String department, BigDecimal salary) { }
```

### How does reduce with `maxBy` select an extreme step by step?

Reduce starts with the first element as the running result, then at each step hands the running result and the next element to `maxBy`, which returns the greater. The running result is therefore always the maximum seen so far, and after the last element it is the overall maximum.

```java
// Conceptually, for salaries [1200000, 850000, 2000000]:
// step 1: max(1200000, 850000)  = 1200000
// step 2: max(1200000, 2000000) = 2000000  -> final
```

### What is the `Stream.max` shorthand?

`Stream.max(comparator)` is a dedicated terminal that returns the maximum element wrapped in an `Optional`. It expresses the intent directly and is the clearest choice in most code; under the hood it does the same pairwise selection as reduce with `maxBy`.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import com.bank.model.Employee;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class HighestPaidMax {
    public Optional<Employee> highestPaid(List<Employee> employees) {
        return employees.stream().max(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary));
    }
}
```

```text
Optional[Employee[id=3, name=Pankaj, ...]]
```

### What is the `Collectors.maxBy` collector form?

`Collectors.maxBy(comparator)` is a collector that produces the maximum element as an `Optional`. On its own it is just another way to write `Stream.max`, but its real value is as a downstream collector inside `groupingBy`, where it yields the maximum per group.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import com.bank.model.Employee;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Optional;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingBy;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.maxBy;

public class TopEarnerPerDepartment {
    public Map<String, Optional<Employee>> topPerDept(List<Employee> employees) {
        return employees.stream()
                .collect(groupingBy(Employee::department,
                        maxBy(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary))));
    }
}
```

```text
{Management=Optional[Pankaj], Risk=Optional[Priya], Trading=Optional[Rajesh]}
```

### How do the three approaches compare?

| Approach | Form | Returns | Best use |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `reduce(BinaryOperator.maxBy(cmp))` | terminal | `Optional<T>` | shows the mechanism; composes with other operators |
| `Stream.max(cmp)` | terminal | `Optional<T>` | the clearest choice for a plain maximum |
| `Collectors.maxBy(cmp)` | collector | `Optional<T>` | downstream inside `groupingBy` for per-group max |

```java
// All three produce the same highest-paid employee
Comparator<Employee> cmp = Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary);
Optional<Employee> a = employees.stream().reduce(BinaryOperator.maxBy(cmp));
Optional<Employee> b = employees.stream().max(cmp);
Optional<Employee> c = employees.stream().collect(Collectors.maxBy(cmp));
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is calling `.get()` on the result without handling an empty stream, which throws `NoSuchElementException`.

```java
// WRONG: get on a possibly-empty result
Employee top = employees.stream().max(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary)).get();
// NoSuchElementException when employees is empty
```

```java
// CORRECT: handle the empty case
Employee top = employees.stream()
        .max(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary))
        .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalStateException("no employees to rank"));
```

The second mistake is using `reduce` with an identity that is not a real neutral element, which corrupts the result. For finding a maximum, prefer the no-identity `reduce` that returns an `Optional`, or `Stream.max`.

```java
// WRONG: a fake identity can win incorrectly or skew the fold
Employee sentinel = new Employee(0, "", "", new BigDecimal("-1"));
Employee top = employees.stream()
        .reduce(sentinel, BinaryOperator.maxBy(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary)));
// returns the sentinel for an empty stream, which the caller may not expect
```

```java
// CORRECT: no identity, get an Optional that is empty when there is nothing
Optional<Employee> top = employees.stream()
        .reduce(BinaryOperator.maxBy(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary)));
```

### What are edge cases, parallelism, and the `minBy` mirror?

All three return an `Optional` because an empty stream has no maximum, so the caller must handle emptiness. For ties, these operations keep one element per their defined behavior, so the comparator should be consistent to make the choice predictable. Because `maxBy` is associative, reduce and max parallelize correctly, giving the same result sequentially or in parallel.

```java
// Parallel gives the same maximum because maxBy is associative
Optional<Employee> top = employees.parallelStream()
        .reduce(BinaryOperator.maxBy(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary)));
```

`BinaryOperator.minBy` mirrors `maxBy` to select the minimum, and `Stream.min` is its shorthand.

```java
// Lowest-paid employee
Optional<Employee> lowest = employees.stream()
        .reduce(BinaryOperator.minBy(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary)));
Optional<Employee> lowestShort = employees.stream()
        .min(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary));
```

### When should you use each, and what are the follow-ups?

Prefer `Stream.max` for a plain maximum because it reads clearly. Use `Collectors.maxBy` when you need the maximum as a downstream inside `groupingBy`, such as the top earner per department. Reach for `reduce` with `maxBy` when you want to show or reason about the underlying mechanism, or when you compose the operator with other reductions. Interviewers often ask why all three return an `Optional` (an empty stream has no maximum), how to get the per-group maximum (`groupingBy` with `maxBy` downstream), and whether these parallelize safely (yes, because the operator is associative).
